Below are representative JUnit5 + Mockito test methods (no class boilerplate) — three for TaskService and two for TaskController.

Service tests (mock TaskRepository):
```java
// 1) no-status -> calls findByUserIdOrderByCreatedAtDesc
@Test
void listTasksForUser_noStatus_callsFindByUserId() {
    TaskRepository repo = mock(TaskRepository.class);
    TaskService svc = new TaskService(repo);
    List<Task> expected = List.of(new Task());
    when(repo.findByUserIdOrderByCreatedAtDesc("user1")).thenReturn(expected);

    List<Task> result = svc.listTasksForUser("user1");

    assertSame(expected, result);
    verify(repo).findByUserIdOrderByCreatedAtDesc("user1");
    verify(repo, never()).findByUserIdAndStatusOrderByCreatedAtDesc(any(), any());
}

// 2) valid status -> parses and calls findByUserIdAndStatusOrderByCreatedAtDesc
@Test
void listTasksForUser_validStatus_parsesAndCallsRepo() {
    TaskRepository repo = mock(TaskRepository.class);
    TaskService svc = new TaskService(repo);
    TaskStatus s = TaskStatus.IN_PROGRESS;
    List<Task> expected = List.of(new Task());
    when(repo.findByUserIdAndStatusOrderByCreatedAtDesc("user1", s)).thenReturn(expected);

    List<Task> result = svc.listTasksForUser("user1", "IN_PROGRESS");

    assertSame(expected, result);
    verify(repo).findByUserIdAndStatusOrderByCreatedAtDesc("user1", s);
}

// 3) invalid status -> return empty list and don't touch repo
@Test
void listTasksForUser_invalidStatus_returnsEmptyList() {
    TaskRepository repo = mock(TaskRepository.class);
    TaskService svc = new TaskService(repo);

    List<Task> result = svc.listTasksForUser("user1", "UNKNOWN_STATUS");

    assertTrue(result.isEmpty());
    verifyNoInteractions(repo);
}
```

Controller tests (MockMvc standalone with mocked TaskService):
```java
// 4) controller with no status -> delegates with null and returns 200 + list
@Test
void controller_noStatus_delegatesWithNull() throws Exception {
    TaskService svc = mock(TaskService.class);
    TaskController ctrl = new TaskController(svc, /*mapper*/ null);
    MockMvc mvc = MockMvcBuilders.standaloneSetup(ctrl).build();
    when(svc.listTasksForUser("user1", null)).thenReturn(List.of());

    mvc.perform(get("/api/tasks").principal(() -> "user1"))
       .andExpect(status().isOk())
       .andExpect(content().json("[]"));

    verify(svc).listTasksForUser("user1", null);
}

// 5) controller with invalid status -> returns 200 + empty list
@Test
void controller_invalidStatus_returnsEmpty200() throws Exception {
    TaskService svc = mock(TaskService.class);
    TaskController ctrl = new TaskController(svc, /*mapper*/ null);
    MockMvc mvc = MockMvcBuilders.standaloneSetup(ctrl).build();
    when(svc.listTasksForUser("user1", "BAD")).thenReturn(Collections.emptyList());

    mvc.perform(get("/api/tasks").param("status", "BAD").principal(() -> "user1"))
       .andExpect(status().isOk())
       .andExpect(content().json("[]"));

    verify(svc).listTasksForUser("user1", "BAD");
}
```
These tests show the intended wiring: the old service method remains (delegates to the new overload), valid status strings map to TaskStatus and call the status-aware repo method, and invalid status yields an empty list / 200 from the controller.
